{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I burned dinner again and my partner is home in an hour. I feel like I can't do anything right lately.", "response": "Oh, I hear how hard you are being on yourself right now. One burnt dinner does not erase everything else you bring to your life. Right now, order something nice from that place you both love, or keep it simple with sandwiches. Either way, your partner loves you for who you are, not for your cooking skills. Be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend in this spot."}
{"prompt": "I've been working from home for a year now and I feel so isolated. Everyone at the office seems to have moved on without me.", "response": "That kind of loneliness is real and valid, even if you know logically that people have their own struggles. You are not invisible just because you are not in the room. Try reaching out to one colleague for a virtual coffee — most people miss those conversations too and will be genuinely happy to catch up. Also, consider finding one weekly in-person routine, even something small like a Saturday morning book club or a walk at a regular cafe. Connection grows from tiny seeds."}
{"prompt": "My teenager slammed their door and has been giving me the silent treatment for days. I'm worried but I also don't want to push.", "response": "This is such a tough season — the part where they need space but you need connection. You are right not to push; teenagers often test boundaries to figure out who they are, and it usually is not about you personally. Leave a small note with no demands, maybe something like 'Lunch is in the fridge if you're hungry' or 'No need to reply, just thinking of you.' Keep the door open, literally and figuratively. Your steady, non-reactive love is exactly what they need, even when they pretend otherwise."}
{"prompt": "I just turned 35 and I feel like I've fallen behind everyone else. My friends have houses and promotions and I still don't know what I want to do with my life.", "response": "Take a gentle breath. First, I want you to notice something: the lives you see on the surface are often not the full picture. People carry their own doubts and pressures, even when they look 'figured out.' Second, thirty-five is not late for anything — it is a perfectly ordinary age for reinvention. What matters is not whether you match an imaginary timeline, but whether you are moving toward something that feels true to you. Start with a small question: what made you feel alive or engaged in the last year? Follow that thread."}
{"prompt": "I accidentally sent a very negative email about my coworker to them instead of to my friend. I want to hide under a rock.", "response": "Oh no — and first, let me say, this has happened to far more people than you think. The shame will fade, but for now, the kindest thing you can do is own it quickly and simply. Reply with something like, 'I am so sorry, that email was sent to you by mistake and it does not reflect how I feel about you or your work.' Then leave it at that. Most people understand these digital mishaps. You do not need to explain further. Give them space, be your normal, supportive self going forward, and try to forgive yourself. We are all human."}
